============================================================
MECHANISM
============================================================
accepted B staging: per-thread synchronous vectorized global loads (LDG float4)
                     + STS into Bs, guarded by __syncthreads each K-tile.
candidate B staging: single-thread-issued UTMALDG.3D (TMA) bulk copy into Bs,
                     completed via mbarrier (256 arrivals + 4096 tx bytes).
TMA instruction verified: YES (UTMALDG.3D in SASS).

============================================================
PERFORMANCE (2 batches x 15 samples, interleaved, same machine state)
============================================================
Run A (clean):
  accepted combined median = 0.270672 ms   (min 0.2600, max 0.3553)
  tma-b    combined median = 0.268864 ms   (min 0.2603, max 0.3742)
  delta                          = -0.67 %   (tma faster)
  batch1: acc 0.268544 vs tma 0.266176 ; batch2: acc 0.270688 vs tma 0.270048
Run B (noisy, outliers to ~2.3 ms):
  accepted combined = 0.277424 ms ; tma-b combined = 0.278032 ms ; delta +0.22 %
=> Direction flips sign run-to-run; both within the ~1% noise band.
   Per gate: <=~1% or inconsistent => REJECT / NOISE.

============================================================
VERDICT: REJECT / NOISE
============================================================
B-only TMA did NOT measurably improve the accepted kernel. The B staging is a
small fraction of runtime; without software pipelining the block simply waits on
the mbarrier each tile (no overlap), so TMA is not expected to beat the
synchronous path. This does NOT conclude all TMA is useless — it only
establishes that swapping B's transport alone is not a repeatable win.
Numerical behavior is bit-identical (correctness fully preserved).

